Human English claims that a map of several variables between complete lattices is jointly continuous iff it is separately continuous. For two variables, the product is a complete lattice whose induced topology is the product topology, projections are continuous so joint continuity yields separate continuity, and conversely if \(f(\bigsqcup S,y)=\bigsqcup_x f(x,y)\) and \(f(x,\bigsqcup S')=\bigsqcup_y f(x,y)\), then for directed \(S^*\subseteq D\times D'\) with projections \(S,S'\) one has \(\bigsqcup S^*=(\bigsqcup S,\bigsqcup S')\), separate continuity gives a supremum of values \(f(x,y)\) over \(x\in S\) and \(y\in S'\), and directedness plus monotonicity replaces that index set by \(S^*\). The AI English of `proposition_2_6` establishes the same equivalence as preservation of directed suprema of the joint map iff every slice preserves them, with continuity read as that preservation, two variables enough, and the product carrying the componentwise lattice structure whose induced topology is the product topology. Joint preservation restricts to a slice because the image of a nonempty directed set under the slice embedding is nonempty and directed and its supremum is the sliced pair (one projection recovers the set, the other is a singleton). Conversely, separate preservation gives monotone slices and a monotone joint map; for nonempty directed \(S^*\) both projections are nonempty and directed and the supremum is the pair of suprema; preservation in each variable writes \(f\) of that pair as a supremum of values \(f(x,y)\); directedness supplies a common upper bound in \(S^*\) for witnesses of \(x\) and of \(y\), monotonicity puts \(f(x,y)\) below the image of that bound, and the opposite inequality is monotonicity on members of \(S^*\). Checked: that sandwich is the English passage from the double supremum over \(S\times S'\) to the supremum over \(S^*\). **Theorem:** yes — joint and separate continuity agree. **Proof:** yes — slice embeddings one way, and project